 ## Shared principles
-The method relies on the mathematical property of the Signed Distance Function (SDF). An SDF $f(\mathbf{p})$ maps a 3D point $\mathbf{p}$ to the shortest Euclidean distance to the nearest surface of the scene geometry [S2, S5, unofficial]. The sign indicates position: $f(\mathbf{p}) < 0$ inside, $f(\mathbf{p}) = 0$ on the surface, and $f(\mathbf{p}) > 0$ outside [S2, S5, S9643].
+The method operates on the principle that a Signed Distance Function $f(\mathbf{p})$ provides a lower bound on the distance to the nearest surface. For any point $\mathbf{p}$ in space, $f(\mathbf{p})$ returns the shortest Euclidean distance to the implicit surface, with a sign indicating position: $f(\mathbf{p}) < 0$ inside, $f(\mathbf{p}) = 0$ on the surface, and $f(\mathbf{p}) > 0$ outside [G].
 
